WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.177
This slide covers one of the most important ideas in the module.

00:00:05.177 --> 00:00:12.142
In an outer join, placing a condition in ON can produce a very different population from placing the

00:00:12.142 --> 00:00:15.772
same condition in WHERE. Look at the example on the left.

00:00:15.772 --> 00:00:19.972
We want every patient, but among lab records we only want Glucose tests to match.

00:00:19.972 --> 00:00:22.917
The Glucose condition is inside ON.

00:00:22.917 --> 00:00:26.722
Ana has a Glucose test, so that test matches.

00:00:26.722 --> 00:00:34.172
Ben and Cara do not have a matching Glucose row, but they remain because the LEFT JOIN still

00:00:34.172 --> 00:00:36.332
preserves every patient. Their lab values become NULL.

00:00:36.332 --> 00:00:40.622
Now look at the example on the right.

00:00:40.622 --> 00:00:44.477
The LEFT JOIN happens first, and then WHERE requires the lab test to equal Glucose.

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:49.877
Ben and Cara have either a non-Glucose value or a NULL lab value,

00:00:49.877 --> 00:00:52.677
so those rows fail the final filter.

00:00:52.677 --> 00:00:56.887
Only Ana remains. The rule is simple but powerful.

00:00:56.887 --> 00:01:00.467
ON controls which rows are eligible to match.

00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:05.997
WHERE filters the result after the join has produced matched and unmatched rows.

00:01:05.997 --> 00:01:13.337
Whenever an outer join must preserve a full population, be very careful with conditions on the optional side.

00:01:13.337 --> 00:01:17.810
Ask whether the condition belongs to matching or to final filtering.
